  {
   "type": "page",
   "title": "Beside text",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "figure",
     "figure": "graph",
     "title": "placement: \"beside\"",
     "placement": "beside",
     "domain": {
      "xMin": -3,
      "xMax": 3,
      "yMin": -1,
      "yMax": 9
     },
     "grid": "shown",
     "aspect": "stretch",
     "objects": [
      {
       "type": "function",
       "f": "x^2"
      },
      {
       "type": "points",
       "from": "table",
       "rows": [
        [
         "V",
         0,
         0
        ],
        [
         "P",
         2,
         4
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "caption": "Author intent: figure and prose as one purpose-unit. At width this reads [ prose ][ figure ]; below the point where either column stops working, it stacks.",
     "text": "The curve $y=x^2$ has its vertex at the origin. Reading the plotted points, each $y$ value is the square of its $x$ value — so the curve is symmetric about the $y$-axis and never dips below it. Check $P$ against that rule before moving on."
    }
   ]
  },
